  "id": "DrugBank_DB13928 - MONDO_5148",
  "verbalization": "Semaglutide is associated with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus through a chain of indications that link it to other drugs known to influence glucose regulation. The evidence suggests that Semaglutide may exert a direct or indirect effect on glucose metabolism, as it is indicated for Diabetes Mellitus and is linked to drugs like Gemigliptin, Teneligliptin, and Trelagliptin, which are all classified as incretin mimetics. These drugs work by enhancing insulin secretion and reducing glucagon release, thereby improving glycemic control. The shared indication of Diabetes Mellitus across these drugs implies a potential mechanism by which Semaglutide could influence glucose homeostasis, possibly through similar pathways. The presence of multiple indirect pathways supports the hypothesis that Semaglutide may have a role in the pathophysiology of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
